{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_zqw2s7f5h3tr5myew4cbb43uro4up6jcsbtzxxkvzpbcm4zjg4ya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass, all gates green.\n\n## Result\n\n**48/48 feature combinations pass** (4 backends × 2 thash variants × 6 security params) — symbol parity, valid-path, error-path, and driver stdout.\n\n### Bugs found and fixed in the Rust translation\n\n1. **Missing `#[repr(C)]` on 5 FFI structs** — the highest-impact finding. Rust's default layout reorders fields by descending alignment, so:\n   - `LeafInfoX1` (`u8*, u32, u32*, u32[8], u32[8]`) put `wots_steps` at offset 8 instead of 16\n   - haraka's `SpxCtx` put the 640-byte `tweaked512_rc64` table at offset 0 instead of after the two seeds\n   - `BlakeState256`/`BlakeState512`/`ForsGenLeafInfo` likewise unpinned\n   \n   Every entry point taking these (`SPX_prf_addr`, `SPX_thash`, `SPX_wots_gen_leafx1`, `SPX_wots_treehashx1`, `SPX_merkle_sign`, `blake256_*`…) read the wrong bytes. Invisible to the KAT digest because internal callers use the Rust layout consistently.\n\n2. **`SPX_gen_message_random` (blake) wrote only `SPX_N` bytes** where the C's `blakeX_final(&S, R)` writes the full 32/64-byte digest. `sign.c` hides it (the next bytes are overwritten immediately); an FFI caller sees it.\n\n3. **6 unexported symbols**: `DRBG_ctx`, `AES256_ECB`, `AES256_CTR_DRBG_Update`, `seedexpander`, `seedexpander_init`, `cst`. `DRBG_ctx` had to stop being a private `Mutex<Drbg>` and become the real exported global; `seedexpander` also needed its `x == NULL → -2` check hoisted *before* the length check, as in the C.\n\n### C quirks the Rust must (and does) reproduce\n- `hash_blake.c` passes **byte** counts to `blake*_update`, which takes **bits** — so the BLAKE backend only absorbs `mlen/8` bytes of the message. A corrupted short message can still verify. Documented in `ERRORS.md`; rows 2/5 assert sentinel *equality* plus reachability of `-1` rather than demanding `-1` per corruption.\n- `blake256_update(S, data, 0)` is not a no-op — it wipes `S->buflen` (`blake256.c:327`).\n- `SPX_bytes_to_ull(in, inlen > 8)` shifts by ≥ 64 (C UB); the Rust matches what clang `-O3` produces.\n\n### Artifacts\n`translation/SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md` (40 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (60 rows, all `[x]`), 8 test binaries / ~5.5k lines in `translation/tests/`, and `.verify/` scripts. Both libraries are always reached via `dlopen`/`dlsym` with `RTLD_LOCAL` so the identically-named exports can't interpose — the Rust is never called directly. The 48 KAT driver digests are 48 *distinct* values, confirming each configuration really is a different code path. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":586,"cache_read_input_tokens":389678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":586,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad09d3fe-ada9-47ca-9d56-2e4ee9154101","uuid":"44b7e787-42c4-4161-89bc-58521e0dc24e","timestamp":"2026-09-05T17:29:55.756Z"}
